VALUES ('fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 'dca-security-deep-dive', 'DCA Practice: Container Security Deep Dive', 'docker', ARRAY['DCA', 'Security', 'Docker Content Trust', 'Docker Scout', 'Rootless', 'Secrets']::TEXT[], 38, 'advanced', true, 5, 60, 1, 0);

-- ── Section: Docker Content Trust & Notary Internals (7 questions) — Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com's Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog's retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT's long-standing mechanics as DCA's curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature. ──

VALUES ('5a7f2ae3-9bef-40cf-9fdb-5c403e594d6e', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 1, 'Docker Content Trust''s underlying Notary model uses a hierarchy of signing keys. Which key, generated on the client and never uploaded to the Notary server, forms the root of trust for a repository?', 'Security', 'DCT Key Hierarchy', 'mcq');
VALUES ('5a7f2ae3-9bef-40cf-9fdb-5c403e594d6e', '4174c56b-ae14-475c-b9eb-1257651e4e2e', 'The root key is the ultimate trust anchor, generated and kept client-side (offline, ideally); it signs the root metadata that establishes which other keys (targets, snapshot, timestamp) are trusted for a repository. Note: DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement (full shutdown December 8, 2026) — this tests the model as DCA''s curriculum still examines it.', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

VALUES ('8b07870c-ce60-4db2-b7e0-cffed0f8323e', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 2, 'In the Notary/DCT trust model, what is the timestamp key''s role, and why is it managed server-side rather than by the publisher?', 'Security', 'DCT Timestamp Key', 'mcq');
VALUES ('8b07870c-ce60-4db2-b7e0-cffed0f8323e', '95da87c0-141b-40de-beea-673497f4cd40', 'The timestamp key enables frequent re-signing to prove metadata freshness against freeze/replay attacks; because it must rotate so often, Notary manages it server-side rather than requiring the publisher to sign it manually each time.', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

VALUES ('939645a2-809c-47a9-b7f6-2bdb2738779a', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 3, 'What problem do Notary ''delegation'' keys solve in a team publishing workflow under Docker Content Trust?', 'Security', 'DCT Delegation Keys', 'mcq');
VALUES ('939645a2-809c-47a9-b7f6-2bdb2738779a', '37c59eb5-d3f9-49b6-b39d-c851207fedee', 'Delegations let a repository owner grant scoped signing authority (e.g. to a CI pipeline) without sharing the root/targets key, so a compromised delegate key doesn''t compromise the whole repository''s trust root.', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

VALUES ('acf4fe45-b54e-4b14-8de3-a24b6103bff4', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 4, 'With DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST=1 set in the client''s environment, what happens on `docker pull myregistry/myimage:v2` if that tag was never signed?', 'Security', 'DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST Behavior', 'mcq');
VALUES ('acf4fe45-b54e-4b14-8de3-a24b6103bff4', '315fe479-9d1a-4bda-98b4-01da55690b87', 'DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST=1 makes the client enforce signature verification for both push and pull; pulling an unsigned tag fails outright rather than warning-and-continuing.', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

VALUES ('ac49bc5d-44a0-4cc4-a199-78f7128e6b7d', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 5, 'What does running `docker trust sign myregistry/myimage:v2` actually do, assuming DCT keys already exist for that repository?', 'Security', 'docker trust sign', 'mcq');
VALUES ('ac49bc5d-44a0-4cc4-a199-78f7128e6b7d', '5a9b4caf-30d3-4d86-9223-cd213a40cc88', 'docker trust sign associates a cryptographic signature with the specific manifest digest behind a tag and publishes the updated trust data, so verification later confirms that exact content, not just the tag name.', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

VALUES ('ce808ea6-8b09-4b4d-8dad-1bdf3bfd035a', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 6, 'Docker Content Trust guarantees signed, verifiable provenance for a tag''s content. What does it NOT guarantee about that image?', 'Security', 'DCT Scope of Guarantee', 'mcq');
VALUES ('ce808ea6-8b09-4b4d-8dad-1bdf3bfd035a', 'ef776539-a8be-41af-ae16-5f945ba6d6c3', 'DCT is a provenance/integrity control, not a vulnerability scanner — a maliciously-built but properly-signed image would still pass DCT verification. Vulnerability scanning is a separate concern (see Docker Scout).', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

VALUES ('bb284420-c714-42b5-a87a-c4b7b2777e82', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 7, 'Per Docker''s own 2026 retirement announcement for Docker Content Trust/Notary v1, which of the following is explicitly true about the transition?', 'Security', 'DCT Retirement Impact', 'mcq');
VALUES ('bb284420-c714-42b5-a87a-c4b7b2777e82', '101a23ed-212d-42a4-b459-873e59b05e06', 'Docker''s retirement guidance is explicit that only DCT/Notary-specific trust operations are affected; the described migration path is to OCI-native tools like Sigstore/Cosign or Notation, not a compatible in-place replacement.', 'Security — Docker Content Trust / Notary internals — authored from docs.docker.com''s Content Trust documentation and the official Docker blog''s retirement/migration guidance; verified via web search 2026-09-04. DCT/Notary v1 is mid-retirement as of this build (brownout windows ran July-August 2026; full shutdown December 8, 2026) — questions test DCT''s long-standing mechanics as DCA''s curriculum still examines them, but this is flagged here as (needs verification — recheck against current source) for how long DCA continues to examine a retired feature.');

-- ── Section: Vulnerability Scanning with Docker Scout (8 questions) — Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker's current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material. ──

VALUES ('851dc82e-6b57-4f45-9d98-75c4fc9eb301', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 8, 'What does `docker scout cves myimage:latest` do?', 'Security', 'docker scout cves', 'mcq');
VALUES ('851dc82e-6b57-4f45-9d98-75c4fc9eb301', '3c65514d-e32c-4e5f-afac-0cccf3400542', 'docker scout cves runs Scout''s CVE analysis against the named image (defaulting to the most recently built one), based on the SBOM Scout compiles for it.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('2947d1cd-45ab-4eb0-aad9-b71e820319f3', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 9, 'How does `docker scout quickview` differ from `docker scout cves` in its default output?', 'Security', 'docker scout quickview', 'mcq');
VALUES ('2947d1cd-45ab-4eb0-aad9-b71e820319f3', '130890b2-cf80-43f8-ad27-55ce9b92a6b6', 'quickview is the summary view — vulnerability counts and base-image update suggestions — while cves gives the full, detailed CVE listing.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('45770205-c3bf-4da1-b488-12dfa8a056b4', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 10, 'What is `docker scout compare` used for?', 'Security', 'docker scout compare', 'mcq');
VALUES ('45770205-c3bf-4da1-b488-12dfa8a056b4', '75a4870a-b240-464e-a55e-eea1999786f3', 'compare analyzes two images against each other, surfacing the vulnerability/package delta — useful for confirming a rebuild actually reduced exposure rather than just changing the tag.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('5bf4acb8-f7c7-4258-ae8d-281ba57ac159', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 11, 'What does Docker Scout compile for an image as the basis for its vulnerability matching, before checking any CVE database?', 'Security', 'SBOM Basis', 'mcq');
VALUES ('5bf4acb8-f7c7-4258-ae8d-281ba57ac159', '24ebf8c6-6bf0-4778-9c50-5301a6fa20eb', 'Scout builds an SBOM describing the image''s actual package/component inventory, then matches that inventory against a continuously updated vulnerability database to identify CVEs.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('363c75b1-7691-4d38-ab66-244551f2f8b5', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 12, 'What do the `--epss-score` and `--epss-percentile` flags on `docker scout cves` let you do?', 'Security', 'EPSS Filtering', 'mcq');
VALUES ('363c75b1-7691-4d38-ab66-244551f2f8b5', '969efb77-b50f-4b3b-a75d-1effe160b626', 'EPSS scores estimate real-world exploitation likelihood; filtering by EPSS score/percentile helps prioritize remediation toward CVEs more likely to actually be exploited, a different axis than raw CVSS severity.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('b5a1a60d-53ab-48e2-9630-1dfc5eff4e14', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 13, 'Why would a team export `docker scout cves` results in SARIF format specifically?', 'Security', 'Scout Output Formats', 'mcq');
VALUES ('b5a1a60d-53ab-48e2-9630-1dfc5eff4e14', '01f6369f-22bf-4bf8-9808-cb5dab54f31a', 'SARIF is a widely-supported interchange format for static analysis tooling, letting Scout''s findings flow directly into CI/CD and code-scanning UIs that already consume that format.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('214f7b59-720d-4e4b-b8c9-946644d2e027', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 14, 'A team has both Docker Content Trust and Docker Scout enabled. If an image is properly signed under DCT but Scout reports several critical CVEs in it, what does this indicate?', 'Security', 'Scout vs. DCT Scope', 'mcq');
VALUES ('214f7b59-720d-4e4b-b8c9-946644d2e027', '271caff5-d189-4c4b-85f3-ef7f9a8e9642', 'Signing (DCT) and vulnerability scanning (Scout) are orthogonal controls — a signed image can still contain real vulnerabilities, since DCT proves provenance/integrity, not security posture.', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('83a5095e-08a0-475f-afaa-551a3c53f06f', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 15, 'Docker Scout is the current name for Docker''s official image-analysis and vulnerability-scanning product. What was this capability''s earlier, now-superseded branding?', 'Security', 'Docker Scout Naming History', 'mcq');
VALUES ('83a5095e-08a0-475f-afaa-551a3c53f06f', '7526ec9c-2a59-43ca-9eb3-942d973433d6', 'Docker''s scanning capability was previously branded as docker scan / Docker Security Scanning before being superseded by Docker Scout, which is the current officially-documented product as of this build''s web-search verification (2026-09-04).', 'Security — Docker Scout image scanning — authored from docs.docker.com/scout documentation (CLI reference: docker scout cves/quickview/compare/policy); verified via web search 2026-09-04 that Docker Scout (not the older docker scan / Docker Security Scanning) is Docker''s current official scanning product name, though several Dashboard sub-features (health scores, Scout Everywhere, some integrations) were retired between July-September 2026 — not copied from official Docker exam material.');

-- ── Section: Secrets Management (7 questions) — Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com's Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material. ──

VALUES ('35dfee17-9494-4e11-8d09-2eb6170a45cd', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 16, 'When a Swarm secret is attached to a service, how is it made available inside a task''s container, and why is this considered more secure than an environment variable?', 'Security', 'Secret Delivery Mechanism', 'mcq');
VALUES ('35dfee17-9494-4e11-8d09-2eb6170a45cd', 'f8589af1-20d9-4832-99d6-b9cc9b85ad62', 'Swarm secrets land as files in an in-memory /run/secrets/ mount inside the container — this avoids the common env-var pitfalls (visible in `docker inspect`, leaked into crash dumps/logs, inherited by subprocesses, sometimes visible via /proc).', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('dffd7c03-56c4-44df-aa7e-72b804a8d03c', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 17, 'Beyond visibility in `docker inspect`, what is a concrete risk of passing a database password via `-e DB_PASSWORD=...` on a standalone `docker run` versus a Swarm secret?', 'Security', 'Why Not Env Vars', 'mcq');
VALUES ('dffd7c03-56c4-44df-aa7e-72b804a8d03c', '9a423f54-b018-4126-a6f7-616e3fb0f783', 'The practical risk is exposure surface: env vars proliferate into child processes, /proc, and various logging/monitoring tooling by default, whereas a secret file''s exposure is limited to processes that explicitly read that path.', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('a09f54e5-8769-47fb-afc5-2a0fda8bdf8e', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 18, 'Can a standalone container (started with plain `docker run`, outside of Swarm mode) consume a secret created with `docker secret create`?', 'Security', 'Secrets Are Swarm-Scoped', 'mcq');
VALUES ('a09f54e5-8769-47fb-afc5-2a0fda8bdf8e', '3f6546e8-3f10-4d0a-ab10-d4d175d55845', 'docker secret create/--secret only apply to Swarm services (docker service create/docker stack deploy); there is no standalone `docker run --secret` mechanism.', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('76d60c1a-4cfa-4d8d-b6f8-ae4c4ef1f46b', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 19, 'A Swarm secret''s content cannot be updated in place once created. What is the standard pattern for rotating a secret''s value (e.g. a rotated database password) for a running service?', 'Security', 'Secret Immutability & Rotation', 'mcq');
VALUES ('76d60c1a-4cfa-4d8d-b6f8-ae4c4ef1f46b', 'd86c65c8-c1bb-4a45-bdc0-9c5743ff324c', 'Because secret content is immutable once created, rotation means creating a new secret object and updating the service definition to point at it — there is no in-place `docker secret update` for content.', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('e5d1d879-bc63-4fcb-80ce-e458b528f0ff', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 20, 'By default, what UID/GID and file mode does a mounted secret file have inside the container, and can this be changed?', 'Security', 'Secret File Ownership', 'mcq');
VALUES ('e5d1d879-bc63-4fcb-80ce-e458b528f0ff', 'c46d758c-839c-43ae-81b0-134a12634b64', 'Secrets default to root:root ownership with 0444 permissions, but docker service create exposes --secret-uid/--secret-gid/--secret-mode to customize this for applications that need to read the file as a non-root user.', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('4766afe6-61bc-416e-8333-bff97d5bb182', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 21, 'Swarm also has a `docker config create` resource, distinct from secrets. What is the key functional difference between a Swarm ''config'' and a Swarm ''secret''?', 'Security', 'Configs vs. Secrets', 'mcq');
VALUES ('4766afe6-61bc-416e-8333-bff97d5bb182', '1e8cfde7-127b-465a-92ab-f2e6e24a8ced', 'Configs and secrets share a similar mounting mechanism, but configs are designed for non-sensitive configuration data (and, unlike secrets, can be inspected via `docker config inspect` without redaction) — secrets are the mechanism intended for actual sensitive material.', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('86502036-9337-4c3b-82c5-0b10220ed293', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 22, 'In a Compose file deployed via `docker stack deploy`, how is a secret typically supplied — via a top-level `secrets:` key referencing a `file:` — actually delivered to Swarm, given Swarm secrets are meant to avoid plaintext-on-disk exposure?', 'Security', 'Secrets in Compose for Stacks', 'mcq');
VALUES ('86502036-9337-4c3b-82c5-0b10220ed293', 'c5ad6008-41ac-41bf-b73d-1b78f5936630', 'For a file-based Compose secret, `docker stack deploy` reads the local file at deploy time and creates a Swarm secret object from its contents; from then on, Swarm''s own secret distribution (not the original file) is what tasks receive.', 'Security — secrets management — authored from docs.docker.com''s Swarm secrets documentation and general container security practice (bind-mounted/env-var secret exposure risks); verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

-- ── Section: Kernel-Level Isolation: seccomp & AppArmor (5 questions) — Security — seccomp/AppArmor/kernel isolation — authored from docs.docker.com's seccomp and AppArmor security documentation; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material. ──

VALUES ('de928c55-f855-403f-bcd1-8dab86aaa1d1', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 23, 'How does supplying a custom seccomp profile via `--security-opt seccomp=/path/to/profile.json` differ from Docker''s default seccomp profile?', 'Security', 'Custom seccomp Profiles', 'mcq');
VALUES ('de928c55-f855-403f-bcd1-8dab86aaa1d1', '014f2559-e37f-4030-be3b-87234017334c', 'Docker ships a default seccomp profile blocking a substantial syscall set already; a custom profile lets you tune this further per-workload, following least-privilege (though a badly written custom profile could also loosen restrictions).', 'Security — seccomp/AppArmor/kernel isolation — authored from docs.docker.com''s seccomp and AppArmor security documentation; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('fe99344e-05b0-4db6-9eaf-b66e619ef6dc', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 24, 'What does `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined` do, and what is the security implication?', 'Security', 'Disabling seccomp', 'mcq');
VALUES ('fe99344e-05b0-4db6-9eaf-b66e619ef6dc', 'e83cfd27-16ba-4d71-affe-edfcfd0f1cfc', 'unconfined removes the seccomp filter entirely, expanding the syscall surface exposed to the container — generally discouraged except for specific, well-understood cases (e.g. certain debugging/tracing tools).', 'Security — seccomp/AppArmor/kernel isolation — authored from docs.docker.com''s seccomp and AppArmor security documentation; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('932faf59-b35b-42e0-95af-06085b777df2', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 25, 'AppArmor and seccomp both provide kernel-level restriction, but at different granularities. What is the key conceptual difference between them?', 'Security', 'AppArmor vs. seccomp', 'mcq');
VALUES ('932faf59-b35b-42e0-95af-06085b777df2', '87cb2ceb-23c4-46c1-b911-e89c482890e9', 'seccomp is a syscall filter; AppArmor is a path/resource-based MAC system layered on top, restricting what a confined process can do with files, capabilities, and network access beyond the syscall level.', 'Security — seccomp/AppArmor/kernel isolation — authored from docs.docker.com''s seccomp and AppArmor security documentation; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('19033d80-8653-431a-93ec-5964c4ca69b2', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 26, 'On a Docker host with AppArmor enabled (e.g. Ubuntu), what happens to a container by default if no custom AppArmor profile is specified?', 'Security', 'Docker''s Default AppArmor Profile', 'mcq');
VALUES ('19033d80-8653-431a-93ec-5964c4ca69b2', 'e91cfba4-2258-41c0-b386-7b37bcacfa16', 'Docker automatically generates and applies a docker-default AppArmor profile to containers on AppArmor-enabled hosts unless a different profile (or `unconfined`) is explicitly requested.', 'Security — seccomp/AppArmor/kernel isolation — authored from docs.docker.com''s seccomp and AppArmor security documentation; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('ddfe013e-8c2c-456e-afed-e5e92b27af1e', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 27, 'Is it valid and typical to run a container with BOTH a custom seccomp profile AND a custom AppArmor profile applied simultaneously?', 'Security', 'Combining seccomp and AppArmor', 'mcq');
VALUES ('ddfe013e-8c2c-456e-afed-e5e92b27af1e', '1706b8a2-e451-4ade-8448-0f14e54eb2fb', 'seccomp and AppArmor are complementary, independent layers; combining a tailored seccomp profile with a tailored AppArmor profile (and dropped capabilities) is standard container-hardening practice, not a conflict.', 'Security — seccomp/AppArmor/kernel isolation — authored from docs.docker.com''s seccomp and AppArmor security documentation; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

-- ── Section: Rootless Docker & User Namespace Remapping (5 questions) — Security — rootless mode & user namespace remapping — authored from docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless and docs.docker.com/engine/security/userns-remap (rootless doc updated 2026-06, userns-remap doc updated 2026-04, both re-verified via web search 2026-09-04); not copied from official Docker exam material. ──

VALUES ('8926adf9-0f28-4b05-bcf1-ae947f7054ac', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 28, 'What is the key difference between running Docker in ''rootless mode'' and enabling ''userns-remap'' on a conventionally-installed (root) daemon?', 'Security', 'Rootless vs. userns-remap', 'mcq');
VALUES ('8926adf9-0f28-4b05-bcf1-ae947f7054ac', 'bdfe433c-f27b-4e81-9de5-67c49747c108', 'docs.docker.com''s own comparison states this explicitly: userns-remap keeps a root daemon but remaps container UIDs, while rootless mode removes root privileges from the daemon itself as well as the containers.', 'Security — rootless mode & user namespace remapping — authored from docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless and docs.docker.com/engine/security/userns-remap (rootless doc updated 2026-06, userns-remap doc updated 2026-04, both re-verified via web search 2026-09-04); not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('a1f3fdec-e49e-4c19-a554-cf031f1c6ef4', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 29, 'Can userns-remap be enabled together with rootless mode on the same Docker installation?', 'Security', 'Rootless + userns-remap Compatibility', 'mcq');
VALUES ('a1f3fdec-e49e-4c19-a554-cf031f1c6ef4', 'b1f70fe5-68ad-4d68-b8d9-ff45cda17fbd', 'Current Docker documentation and the moby/moby issue tracker confirm userns-remap is not supported under rootless mode — the two features address overlapping concerns via different, non-composable mechanisms.', 'Security — rootless mode & user namespace remapping — authored from docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless and docs.docker.com/engine/security/userns-remap (rootless doc updated 2026-06, userns-remap doc updated 2026-04, both re-verified via web search 2026-09-04); not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('72d324b7-919c-4d1f-ae13-bfd080b1554c', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 30, 'Running Docker in rootless mode comes with some documented functional trade-offs compared to a conventional root daemon. Which of these is a real, documented rootless-mode limitation?', 'Security', 'Rootless Mode Limitations', 'mcq');
VALUES ('72d324b7-919c-4d1f-ae13-bfd080b1554c', 'f8182a1d-d691-47dd-a83a-2156448fc38a', 'Rootless mode''s documented limitations include restrictions around certain network drivers, cgroup-based resource controls depending on the host''s cgroup setup, and binding privileged ports without additional configuration (e.g. slirp4netns/rootlesskit port forwarding, CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE workarounds).', 'Security — rootless mode & user namespace remapping — authored from docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless and docs.docker.com/engine/security/userns-remap (rootless doc updated 2026-06, userns-remap doc updated 2026-04, both re-verified via web search 2026-09-04); not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('d5308355-90bf-4da2-aeee-d16d98c67b88', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 31, 'Mechanically, what does enabling userns-remap actually change about how a container''s root user (UID 0 inside the container) is represented on the host?', 'Security', 'userns-remap Mechanism', 'mcq');
VALUES ('d5308355-90bf-4da2-aeee-d16d98c67b88', '02307d9c-287a-4314-a219-e82ca9155ec0', 'userns-remap maps the container''s UID/GID range (including its apparent ''root'', UID 0) onto an unprivileged range of host UIDs/GIDs, so container-root escaping to the host doesn''t grant real host-root.', 'Security — rootless mode & user namespace remapping — authored from docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless and docs.docker.com/engine/security/userns-remap (rootless doc updated 2026-06, userns-remap doc updated 2026-04, both re-verified via web search 2026-09-04); not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('00e6cbdb-712f-4343-852f-2bd9a16fd770', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 32, 'From a threat-model perspective, what specific class of attack does userns-remap primarily mitigate that Docker''s default (non-remapped) configuration does not?', 'Security', 'Why This Matters for Container Breakout', 'mcq');
VALUES ('00e6cbdb-712f-4343-852f-2bd9a16fd770', '6e6f0593-37bf-4c08-b5d6-feac6b68e21a', 'userns-remap specifically narrows the blast radius of a kernel/runtime-level container-escape: what would otherwise be a host-root compromise becomes a compromise of an unprivileged host account instead.', 'Security — rootless mode & user namespace remapping — authored from docs.docker.com/engine/security/rootless and docs.docker.com/engine/security/userns-remap (rootless doc updated 2026-06, userns-remap doc updated 2026-04, both re-verified via web search 2026-09-04); not copied from official Docker exam material.');

-- ── Section: TLS for the Docker Daemon & Registry (6 questions) — Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com's 'Protect the Docker daemon socket' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material. ──

VALUES ('7bfa67c8-5162-4f0d-acfe-c3d210ab44b6', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 33, 'By default, if the Docker daemon is configured to listen on a TCP socket (e.g. `-H tcp://0.0.0.0:2375`) with no additional configuration, what is the security implication?', 'Security', 'Unauthenticated Daemon Socket Risk', 'mcq');
VALUES ('7bfa67c8-5162-4f0d-acfe-c3d210ab44b6', 'bc30e85f-69f5-48cd-bbac-6e41909ed783', 'Port 2375 (plain TCP, no TLS) is explicitly documented as insecure — anyone reaching it gets unauthenticated daemon control, which is effectively root on the host given what the Docker API can do (e.g. bind-mounting `/`).', 'Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com''s ''Protect the Docker daemon socket'' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('7f3f5b55-c6d5-4fa1-98b8-19692831e9f4', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 34, 'What TCP port is the conventional default for a TLS-secured Docker daemon socket, distinct from the insecure plain-TCP default?', 'Security', 'Daemon TLS Port Convention', 'mcq');
VALUES ('7f3f5b55-c6d5-4fa1-98b8-19692831e9f4', '8435b583-581f-4865-bd50-eae6a1e19dc2', '2375 is the conventional plain (insecure) daemon port; 2376 is the conventional TLS-secured daemon port — using 2376 alone doesn''t secure anything without also actually configuring TLS certs (--tlsverify and related flags).', 'Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com''s ''Protect the Docker daemon socket'' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('bab80b80-cc62-499f-8b09-3e10e2a3738c', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 35, 'Configuring the daemon with `--tlsverify` (plus --tlscacert, --tlscert, --tlskey) does what specifically, beyond just encrypting the connection?', 'Security', 'Daemon TLS Client Verification', 'mcq');
VALUES ('bab80b80-cc62-499f-8b09-3e10e2a3738c', '2ea8297d-4252-41cd-a6a4-2c5d724bede3', '--tlsverify enables mutual TLS: the daemon requires a client certificate signed by the configured CA, so encryption alone isn''t the point — unauthorized clients are rejected outright.', 'Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com''s ''Protect the Docker daemon socket'' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('5850ef15-3f27-4a7f-8de6-1a0eba787c90', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 36, 'By default, will the Docker client refuse to push/pull from a private registry served over plain HTTP (no TLS)?', 'Security', 'Registry TLS Requirement', 'mcq');
VALUES ('5850ef15-3f27-4a7f-8de6-1a0eba787c90', '71916b5a-3afe-484e-bb78-e7e6a3936691', 'Docker refuses non-TLS registries by default; an operator must explicitly list a registry under insecure-registries in the daemon config to permit plain-HTTP communication with it — an explicit, auditable opt-out.', 'Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com''s ''Protect the Docker daemon socket'' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('711a1130-24cf-4e8c-8d24-7aa82ada2c9a', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 37, 'A private registry uses a certificate signed by an internal/self-signed CA rather than a public CA. What must be done on each Docker host that needs to pull from it, without marking the registry as ''insecure''?', 'Security', 'Self-Signed Registry Certs', 'mcq');
VALUES ('711a1130-24cf-4e8c-8d24-7aa82ada2c9a', 'ae4bd138-3e83-4206-965f-e69b7000c830', 'Docker supports per-registry CA trust via certs.d/<host:port>/ca.crt, letting an internal CA be trusted for a specific registry without weakening TLS verification for every other registry (which marking it ''insecure'' would effectively do).', 'Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com''s ''Protect the Docker daemon socket'' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');

VALUES ('a8c29688-21cd-42ea-bbd5-593699a49f93', 'fa1476d0-ee2c-4111-ad5f-6ed4bc605a32', 38, 'Beyond a registry presenting a valid server certificate, some registries also require client certificates for pull/push (mutual TLS). Where does the Docker client look for a matching client cert/key pair for a given registry host?', 'Security', 'mTLS for Registry Access', 'mcq');
VALUES ('a8c29688-21cd-42ea-bbd5-593699a49f93', 'f5de2659-1bd7-42d5-82d7-eb6f6e18e088', 'The same per-registry certs.d/<host:port>/ directory holds both the CA cert (for verifying the registry) and, if required, a client .cert/.key pair (for the registry to verify the client) — registry TLS config is host-scoped under that path.', 'Security — TLS for the Docker daemon and registry — authored from docs.docker.com''s ''Protect the Docker daemon socket'' documentation and standard registry TLS practice; verified via web search 2026-09-04; not copied from official Docker exam material.');
